1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:05,080
If you get a pop-up that offers you 10%
off, and the button to close it says,

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:08,620
"No thanks, I hate saving money,"
there's a name for that thing.

3
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:13,105
It's called a dark pattern. A dark
pattern is a design in an app or a website

4
00:00:13,125 --> 00:00:16,415
that tricks you into doing
something that you don't mean to do,

5
00:00:16,465 --> 00:00:21,837
like buying something extra. Now, a dark
pattern is different from bad design.

6
00:00:21,857 --> 00:00:24,877
Most of the time, dark
patterns are intentional.

7
00:00:24,897 --> 00:00:28,737
The dark pattern usually helps the
business at the expense of the user.

8
00:00:28,757 --> 00:00:31,557
There are a lot of dark patterns
that people use to build stuff,

9
00:00:31,577 --> 00:00:33,457
and I'll go over a lot
of them in this video.

10
00:00:33,477 --> 00:00:37,057
Most of all of the stuff I'm using in
this video come from Harry Brignull,

11
00:00:37,077 --> 00:00:40,497
who is a UX designer, and he was the
one that coined the term "dark patterns"

12
00:00:40,517 --> 00:00:41,217
in

13
00:00:41,237 --> 00:00:42,557
2010. I'll put

14
00:00:42,917 --> 00:00:46,813
the link to his website below. The
first one is the confirmshaming.

15
00:00:47,715 --> 00:00:53,215
confirmshaming is when the button to say
"No" is written to make the user feel

16
00:00:53,235 --> 00:00:56,135
bad. Like, look at this.
No, I hate saving money.

17
00:00:56,155 --> 00:01:00,415
The idea is that you'd rather click
yes than say you hate saving money.

18
00:01:00,435 --> 00:01:02,775
Another pattern is hard to cancel.

19
00:01:02,795 --> 00:01:04,935
People used to call it the roach motel.

20
00:01:04,955 --> 00:01:08,895
The name comes from a cockroach
trap where it's easy to get in,

21
00:01:08,915 --> 00:01:12,095
but it's hard to get out. A lot
of people can relate to this,

22
00:01:12,115 --> 00:01:14,575
where joining or subscribing is easy.

23
00:01:14,595 --> 00:01:17,295
But leaving and/or unsubscribing is hard.

24
00:01:17,315 --> 00:01:20,435
You join an online shop's delivery
membership with one click.

25
00:01:20,455 --> 00:01:25,015
But to cancel, you have to go through
page after page after page of offers,

26
00:01:25,035 --> 00:01:30,105
You know, like this, we also have another
dark pattern called sneak into basket

27
00:01:30,167 --> 00:01:31,167
is when

28
00:01:31,187 --> 00:01:33,557
something you didn't choose
end up in your shopping cart.

29
00:01:33,987 --> 00:01:37,447
So say you add a pair of shoes
and now you get to check out.

30
00:01:37,467 --> 00:01:41,967
There is also a cleaning spray for
five euro that you never picked.

31
00:01:41,987 --> 00:01:46,267
Another interesting dark pattern is hidden
costs where there are fees that only

32
00:01:46,287 --> 00:01:48,127
show up at the last step of checkout.

33
00:01:48,147 --> 00:01:52,327
This happens to me a lot. You think that
the total of your cart is 90 euro and

34
00:01:52,347 --> 00:01:54,787
now you are at checkout and
You're seeing 100 and something.

35
00:01:54,807 --> 00:01:57,327
The shoe cost 40 euro on the product page.

36
00:01:57,347 --> 00:02:01,607
And at the last page, you see that a
service fee has been added and is now 52

37
00:02:01,627 --> 00:02:04,547
euros. And by then you've
typed in your card details,

38
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:06,847
so it just feels easier to just pay now.

39
00:02:06,867 --> 00:02:12,347
Another dark pattern is hidden subscription,
which is like a free trial that

40
00:02:12,367 --> 00:02:15,247
turns into a paid plan
without a clear warning.

41
00:02:15,267 --> 00:02:19,527
So a shop gives you a free month of
premium, but it asks for your card first,

42
00:02:19,547 --> 00:02:21,987
which is now is a dark
pattern just so you know.

43
00:02:22,007 --> 00:02:25,327
And when that free trial ends,
they just start charging you.

44
00:02:25,347 --> 00:02:26,327
This has happened to me before.

45
00:02:26,347 --> 00:02:28,527
They literally charged me
for a year's subscription.

46
00:02:28,547 --> 00:02:31,427
There are many more dark patterns that
you will run into when you use apps or

47
00:02:31,447 --> 00:02:35,007
shops online. And again, keep in mind that
they were intentionally put in place.

48
00:02:35,027 --> 00:02:36,807
It's not bad design, it's intentional.

49
00:02:36,827 --> 00:02:41,047
Another one is trick wording. It's a
question that is written so that you click

50
00:02:41,067 --> 00:02:43,067
the opposite of what
you're meant to click.

51
00:02:43,087 --> 00:02:47,267
Something like, untick this box if you
don't want to stop receiving emails.

52
00:02:47,287 --> 00:02:50,897
You would have to read these two or
three times to work out which one

53
00:02:51,267 --> 00:02:55,747
actually stops the email. One other
common dark pattern is disguised ads.

54
00:02:55,767 --> 00:02:58,967
They are ads that are made to
look like part of the page.

55
00:02:58,987 --> 00:03:04,447
Say you search a shop for running shoes
and the first few results are paid ads

56
00:03:04,467 --> 00:03:07,487
that look exactly like the normal
result, but they are paid ads.

57
00:03:07,507 --> 00:03:11,307
We also have nagging. Nagging is asking
again and again and again after the

58
00:03:11,327 --> 00:03:14,287
user has said no. It's
a type of dark pattern.

59
00:03:14,307 --> 00:03:17,447
Say a shopping app asks you to turn on
notification and you're already saying

60
00:03:17,467 --> 00:03:22,247
no. Next time it asks again. And the
only options are yes and not now.

61
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:25,307
This happens to me a lot. I think
Snapchat does this to me a lot.

62
00:03:25,327 --> 00:03:28,307
I don't want my Snapchat
notifications on, but every time,

63
00:03:28,327 --> 00:03:31,967
every time it's asking me again and again,
I'm this close to deleting the app.

64
00:03:31,987 --> 00:03:35,687
Another one is pre-selection. Pre-selection
is when the option that the company

65
00:03:35,707 --> 00:03:37,667
wants is already ticked for the user.

66
00:03:37,687 --> 00:03:41,927
So there is a box at checkout that
says, add gift wrapping for 2.99 euros,

67
00:03:41,947 --> 00:03:44,247
and it's already ticked. If
you don't read every line,

68
00:03:44,267 --> 00:03:46,647
you pay for gift wrapping
that you never asked for.

69
00:03:46,667 --> 00:03:49,127
Another common dark
pattern is fake urgency.

70
00:03:49,147 --> 00:03:51,507
fake urgency is a deadline
that is not real.

71
00:03:51,807 --> 00:03:56,327
Say a product page shows a countdown
that says, this deal ends in 10 minutes.

72
00:03:56,347 --> 00:03:59,747
If you refresh that page again,
it starts again from 10 minutes.

73
00:03:59,767 --> 00:04:04,167
This deal ends in 10 minutes. It's there
to rush you so you buy before you've

74
00:04:04,187 --> 00:04:05,727
had the time to think about it.

75
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:08,227
Another one is fake scarcity.

76
00:04:08,587 --> 00:04:12,307
This is done mostly by shopping
website, shopping apps,

77
00:04:12,327 --> 00:04:16,087
the website say that there's only a few
left, or that something is selling out.

78
00:04:16,107 --> 00:04:20,270
when it's not true. The page will say,
"Oh, only two left in stock." And when

79
00:04:20,290 --> 00:04:24,310
you come back a week later, it still
says, "Only two left in stock." It's there

80
00:04:24,330 --> 00:04:28,730
to make you buy now, buy now. Why
do companies use dark patterns?

81
00:04:28,750 --> 00:04:32,130
It's, a lot of times, it
comes down to numbers.

82
00:04:32,150 --> 00:04:35,610
Most product teams test their designs
with something called an A and B testing,

83
00:04:35,630 --> 00:04:40,010
A/B testing. Half of the users see
version A, the other half see version B.

84
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:42,890
And whichever one gets more
sales and sign up wins.

85
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:46,050
But an A/B test only measures
the number you pick.

86
00:04:46,070 --> 00:04:48,450
So you can't tell whether
anyone was tricked.

87
00:04:48,550 --> 00:04:52,210
A/B testing usually even doesn't
work for the benefit of the user.

88
00:04:52,230 --> 00:04:54,650
It's just about what design is best.

89
00:04:54,890 --> 00:04:58,310
It doesn't really judge which design
is better or worse for the user.

90
00:04:58,330 --> 00:05:01,630
And I think this is how a lot of
dark patterns end up in apps.

91
00:05:01,650 --> 00:05:04,790
A team is chasing a number,
like, "Oh, we want more sign-ups,

92
00:05:04,810 --> 00:05:07,950
or we want more sales." So it's not
really about what is best for the user.

93
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:12,290
It's about how much the team
can get to their own goal.

94
00:05:12,310 --> 00:05:16,970
How common are dark patterns? In January,
2023, the European Commission checked

95
00:05:17,330 --> 00:05:22,920
399 online stores and 148 of them had at
least one of three dark patterns that

96
00:05:22,970 --> 00:05:27,930
the EU was looking for. 42 of those
websites had fake countdown timers,

97
00:05:27,950 --> 00:05:32,510
and 70 of them were hiding important
information or making important information

98
00:05:32,530 --> 00:05:38,710
hard to see. In July, 2024, consumer and
privacy agencies from about 26 countries,

99
00:05:38,730 --> 00:05:42,410
they looked at over 600 websites
that sell subscriptions,

100
00:05:42,530 --> 00:05:46,707
and they found out that 76% of them
use at least one possible dark pattern,

101
00:05:46,728 --> 00:05:50,108
and nearly 67% of them use
more than one dark pattern.

102
00:05:50,228 --> 00:05:52,928
So what's the law that protects
users against dark patterns?

103
00:05:52,948 --> 00:05:55,458
Are there even laws that protect
users against dark patterns?

104
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:58,540
not a lawyer, but depending on where
you live and everything that I read and

105
00:05:58,560 --> 00:05:59,890
studied, in the EU,

106
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:02,990
the Unfair Commercial
Practices Directive from

107
00:06:03,725 --> 00:06:06,315
2005 already banned some of these tricks,

108
00:06:06,725 --> 00:06:10,485
like tricks like saying a deal is only
available for a very limited time is

109
00:06:10,505 --> 00:06:15,185
banned in the EU. So again protection
from these dark patterns depends on where

110
00:06:15,205 --> 00:06:16,395
you live as a user.

111
00:06:17,265 --> 00:06:19,155
For your data, for example, GDPR

112
00:06:19,865 --> 00:06:24,605
says that a pre-ticked box does not
count as consent on the part of the user.

113
00:06:24,625 --> 00:06:25,605
So if

114
00:06:25,625 --> 00:06:30,245
an online shop charges you for an extra
through a box that they had pre-ticked

115
00:06:30,265 --> 00:06:34,325
or pre-select for you, the EU consumer
law says that you are entitled to get

116
00:06:34,345 --> 00:06:38,155
that money back. In the US, I think
the best known case is the Amazon Prime

117
00:06:38,205 --> 00:06:42,795
case, where the FTC said Amazon signed
people up for Prime without their consent

118
00:06:43,025 --> 00:06:47,965
and made cancelling hard. So in September
2025, Amazon actually did pay 2.5

119
00:06:48,245 --> 00:06:49,705
billion dollars to settle this.

120
00:06:49,725 --> 00:06:50,725
And I

121
00:06:51,325 --> 00:06:55,155
read that the EU is working on this new
law called the Digital Fairness Act.

122
00:06:55,345 --> 00:06:58,645
The idea is that it's expected to deal
with dark patterns more directly.

123
00:06:58,665 --> 00:07:01,905
It hasn't been published yet, but
it's something that is under work.

124
00:07:02,065 --> 00:07:02,765
So

125
00:07:02,785 --> 00:07:06,795
as a user, you just try to keep all of
these patterns in mind as well to protect

126
00:07:06,885 --> 00:07:10,085
yourself. And if you're a developer or a
designer and maybe you work in a company

127
00:07:10,105 --> 00:07:13,385
and you actually see a dark pattern,
I think the way to counter it,

128
00:07:13,405 --> 00:07:15,545
if it's something you would
want to counter, is to be like,

129
00:07:15,565 --> 00:07:18,225
"Hey, we can get sued for
this." Don't even try to say,

130
00:07:18,245 --> 00:07:20,905
"This is wrong," or anything like that,
because maybe you probably wouldn't

131
00:07:20,925 --> 00:07:24,115
be taken seriously. But just be like,
"Hey, guys, we could be sued for this."

132
00:07:24,305 --> 00:07:25,975
I think people take that more seriously.

133
00:07:26,085 --> 00:07:29,267
And try to build the honest version
we can make the world a better place,
